# auditd rules for phase-a-linux endpoints.
# Deployed to /etc/audit/rules.d/talonsoclab.rules, then `augenrules --load`.
#
# In git because /etc/audit/rules.d/ lives on the endpoint, and an endpoint rebuild
# would otherwise silently produce an agent that reports healthy while collecting
# nothing useful -- the same failure class as the ISM policy and the agent group config.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow: execve only. That gives Linux process-creation events
# to pair with Sysmon Event ID 1 on Windows, so the same detection logic can cover both
# platforms in Phase B. Broader file/network auditing is a Phase B decision, not a
# foundation one -- audit volume is easy to turn on and expensive to turn off.

## Exclusions -- MUST come first. auditd rules are first-match, so a `never` rule
## placed after the `always` rules below would have no effect at all.
##
## The container runtime spawns processes constantly (every healthcheck) and none of it
## is security signal on the host: runc and containerd-shim exist to set up namespaces.
## Measured 2026-07-27: 4 of 17 execve records in an idle 60s window, and that share
## grows with every container added.
##
## Deliberate blind spot, stated plainly: execs performed BY the runtime itself are no
## longer recorded. Processes running INSIDE containers still are -- they are ordinary
## execve calls on the shared kernel, and only the runtime binaries are filtered here.
## `curl`, `bash`, `grep` and friends are intentionally NOT excluded; they carry real
## signal and excluding them to save volume would defeat the point of collecting at all.
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F exe=/usr/bin/runc
-a never,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve -F exe=/usr/bin/runc
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F exe=/usr/bin/containerd-shim-runc-v2
-a never,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve -F exe=/usr/bin/containerd-shim-runc-v2
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F exe=/usr/bin/containerd
-a never,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve -F exe=/usr/bin/containerd

## NOT excludable: runc as seen from inside a container's mount namespace, which the
## audit log records as exe="/runc". auditd resolves `-F exe=` against the HOST
## filesystem when the rule loads, `/runc` does not exist there, and the load FAILS --
## `Error sending add rule data request (No such file or directory)`. Worse, that error
## aborts the entire load, so the collection rules below never register either and the
## host silently stops auditing. Verified the hard way 2026-07-27.
## Net effect: host-side runc is filtered, container-namespace runc is not. Accepted.

## Command execution, root and non-root, 64-bit.
-a exit,always -F arch=b64 -F euid=0 -S execve -k audit-wazuh-c
-a exit,always -F arch=b64 -F euid!=0 -S execve -k audit-wazuh-c

## 32-bit equivalents. Cheap to include; a 32-bit binary is otherwise a blind spot.
-a exit,always -F arch=b32 -F euid=0 -S execve -k audit-wazuh-c
-a exit,always -F arch=b32 -F euid!=0 -S execve -k audit-wazuh-c
